WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.583 --> 00:00:02.231
This is Queso.

00:00:02.231 --> 00:00:04.710
And beans does not like to be picked up. 

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:07.382
—Oh, Beans is big!
—This is Beans. Yes.

00:00:07.382 --> 00:00:09.106
Oh, so sweet!

00:00:09.106 --> 00:00:10.000
This is Cameron.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:11.136
He's one of our subscribers.

00:00:11.136 --> 00:00:13.791
He lives in Indiana
with his high school sweetheart.

00:00:13.791 --> 00:00:15.932
And he sent us this question about unions.

00:00:16.353 --> 00:00:20.562
How come we've seen such a decline
in unionization in the U.S.

00:00:20.562 --> 00:00:24.190
and specifically why the unions
from manufacturing

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:26.735
didn't transfer over into services?

00:00:26.735 --> 00:00:28.236
Here's what he's talking about.

00:00:28.236 --> 00:00:29.529
The share of workers in the U.S.

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:33.742
who belong to a labor union
like I do, has fallen to around 10%.

00:00:33.742 --> 00:00:36.719
If you take out the government
employees, it's 6%.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:40.000
And have you ever been part of a union? It sounds like you haven't.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.166
No, I haven't. 

00:00:41.166 --> 00:00:44.586
What got me interested in the question
was just the recent news

00:00:44.586 --> 00:00:46.050
especially around Starbucks.

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:48.088
—"Starbucks workers in upstate New York..."
—"...Santa Cruz...".

00:00:48.088 --> 00:00:49.171
—"...Philedelphia..."
—"...Mesa, Arizona..."

00:00:49.171 --> 00:00:50.327
—"...more than 20..."
—"...30..."

00:00:50.327 --> 00:00:53.052
—"...100..."
—"...200 Starbucks locations..."

00:00:53.052 --> 00:00:56.089
"...voted in favor of joining a union so far."

00:00:56.893 --> 00:01:00.000
Unions have been catching some
momentum here in the U.S.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.740
And if we can make sense
of the historical decline

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:06.733
we might understand what it would take
to reverse it.

00:01:07.636 --> 00:01:09.944
Answering big questions like this
usually involves answering

00:01:09.944 --> 00:01:11.620
a bunch of smaller questions.

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:13.768
And the first one that popped into my head 

00:01:13.782 --> 00:01:17.744
was "Did union density decline
in other countries, too?"

00:01:17.744 --> 00:01:19.079
And the answer is yes.

00:01:19.079 --> 00:01:23.005
Meaning that some of the decline reflects
international trends.

00:01:23.005 --> 00:01:26.503
What happened was that as these economies
grew over time, globalization

00:01:26.503 --> 00:01:28.421
and automation steered job growth

00:01:28.421 --> 00:01:32.656
away from domestic factories
where union membership was high.

00:01:32.656 --> 00:01:35.970
Instead, it went mostly into service
providing jobs where unions weren't

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:37.806
well established.

00:01:37.806 --> 00:01:39.265
All new workplaces in the U.S.

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:40.850
are born non-union.

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:43.603
So when you chart the total number
of union members in the U.S.

00:01:43.603 --> 00:01:47.738
against the huge growth in the workforce,
it looks less like the unions collapsed

00:01:47.738 --> 00:01:50.193
then that the economy
kind of grew outside of them.

00:01:51.611 --> 00:01:55.000
There's a new paper out by
an economist named Zachary Schaller

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:59.198
and it estimates that around 40% of the decline
in union elections in the U.S.

00:01:59.198 --> 00:02:03.359
can be explained by this growth 
and change across sectors.

00:02:03.359 --> 00:02:06.709
But he found that union elections fell
within sectors, too,

00:02:06.709 --> 00:02:10.226
which means that most of the decline
was caused by something else,

00:02:10.226 --> 00:02:13.633
something that explains how we ended up
with even lower union membership

00:02:13.633 --> 00:02:16.261
than other similar countries like Canada.

00:02:18.847 --> 00:02:20.348
Cam, check out this chart.

00:02:20.348 --> 00:02:23.560
It compares union membership in the U.S.

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:25.979
to that of our neighbor up north.

00:02:26.688 --> 00:02:29.440
Okay. So this graph is really interesting.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:34.028
The U.S.'s union density peaked way
earlier than I thought it did.

00:02:34.028 --> 00:02:37.240
Way back in the 1950s,
I thought that was in the eighties.

00:02:38.658 --> 00:02:39.534
Oh, and also

00:02:39.534 --> 00:02:43.538
this paper has a similar chart
that just shows the private sector data.

00:02:43.538 --> 00:02:45.599
So I'll drop that in here too.

00:02:46.416 --> 00:02:48.042
The steepest drops in private sector

00:02:48.042 --> 00:02:51.504
union density came in the 80s,
but it was already falling by then.

00:02:51.504 --> 00:02:56.077
Which made me wonder: what triggered these
steep declines that started in the 1970s?

00:02:56.077 --> 00:02:57.760
I can hear you but I can't see you...

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:00.597
I asked historian Nelson Lichtenstein
to walk me through it.

00:03:01.586 --> 00:03:04.513
In the immediate postwar period.

00:03:04.513 --> 00:03:06.415
You know, in the fifties and sixties,

00:03:06.415 --> 00:03:09.230
there was a sense American capitalism works.

00:03:09.981 --> 00:03:14.157
Unions are functional
to a kind of consumer society.

00:03:14.157 --> 00:03:18.072
"Free enterprise and capitalism,
which the communist despise have given

00:03:18.072 --> 00:03:21.396
the American worker the highest
standard of living in the world."

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:26.164
I mean, everyone's going to have a
suburban house and a Chevy in the driveway.

00:03:26.164 --> 00:03:30.668
And that led to this kind of stolid
complacency.

00:03:30.668 --> 00:03:34.964
Unions negotiated some solid wages
and benefits during the economic boom

00:03:34.964 --> 00:03:36.424
after World War II.

00:03:36.424 --> 00:03:40.966
Their main weight was in the building
trades and manufacturing.

00:03:40.966 --> 00:03:44.140
And frankly, you know, they saw,
you know, what is the typical worker?

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:47.393
Well, sort of some white male,
you know, Midwestern manufacturing.

00:03:47.977 --> 00:03:49.395
There was a kind of, you know, sexism.

00:03:49.395 --> 00:03:51.189
Well, we don't need to organize
these retail clerks.

00:03:51.189 --> 00:03:52.357
They're all, you know, women.

00:03:52.357 --> 00:03:54.150
They're just there for pin money
or something.

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:58.578
So, sure that, yes,
one could properly criticize

00:03:58.578 --> 00:04:00.728
the union movement, especially then.

00:04:02.158 --> 00:04:05.119
If the process of forming a union
is like hiking up a hill

00:04:05.119 --> 00:04:08.746
and the slope of that hill is set
by economic conditions.

00:04:08.746 --> 00:04:11.642
You'd want to make a push
when the unemployment rate is low,

00:04:11.642 --> 00:04:15.678
because going into the 1970s,
that hill got a lot steeper.

00:04:16.965 --> 00:04:18.549
Inflation went through the roof.

00:04:18.549 --> 00:04:19.801
Unemployment jumped.

00:04:19.801 --> 00:04:22.220
Imports were cutting into 
industrial profits.

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:26.140
And employers respond to these pressures
with an anti-union assault.

00:04:26.766 --> 00:04:29.602
They moved factories to the south
to avoid union labor.

00:04:29.602 --> 00:04:33.106
You see an increase in companies
hiring anti-union consulting firms

00:04:33.106 --> 00:04:35.316
and permanently replacing 
striking workers.

00:04:37.093 --> 00:04:40.471
And this strategy gets a boost
from Ronald Reagan in 1981.

00:04:41.197 --> 00:04:43.783
"This morning at 7 a.m.,

00:04:43.783 --> 00:04:46.119
the union representing those
who man Americas

00:04:46.119 --> 00:04:48.736
air traffic control facilities
called a strike."

00:04:49.372 --> 00:04:50.665
Technically, federal employees

00:04:50.665 --> 00:04:53.710
aren't allowed to strike, but that 
hadn't been enforced before.

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:56.015
Well, after the union refused 
a counter offer

00:04:56.015 --> 00:04:59.048
Reagan fired 11,000 striking workers.

00:04:59.465 --> 00:05:01.009
"There just is no other choice."

00:05:01.009 --> 00:05:01.718
It was a signal.

00:05:01.718 --> 00:05:05.638
It was a signal was the first time
the federal government had had been behind

00:05:05.638 --> 00:05:08.850
the destruction of a union
for many decades.

00:05:08.891 --> 00:05:11.192
And every manager in 
the country said

00:05:11.192 --> 00:05:13.890
we can we can do things we couldn't do before.

00:05:14.981 --> 00:05:17.734
Which leads me to ask: what role has government

00:05:17.734 --> 00:05:20.292
policy played in the union decline?

00:05:20.292 --> 00:05:22.447
Here's the process established by U.S. law.

00:05:23.781 --> 00:05:26.928
First, at least 30% of workers at a shop
need to sign a petition

00:05:26.928 --> 00:05:28.244
saying they want a union.

00:05:28.244 --> 00:05:31.205
At this point, their employer
can voluntarily recognize them

00:05:31.205 --> 00:05:32.332
like Vox Media did.

00:05:32.332 --> 00:05:34.584
Otherwise, they go to a 
secret ballot election.

00:05:34.584 --> 00:05:38.296
If a majority of voters says, yes,
the employer is now obligated to bargain

00:05:38.296 --> 00:05:42.425
with the union, but they're not obligated
to ever agree on a contract.

00:05:42.425 --> 00:05:46.971
For those that do, it takes on average
409 days, according to Bloomberg Law.

00:05:47.472 --> 00:05:51.726
I called up sociologist Barry Eidlin to
find out how this is different in Canada.

00:05:52.435 --> 00:05:55.584
If you don't have a contract by the end
of the year in most parts of Canada,

00:05:55.584 --> 00:05:59.359
then the negotiations
get referred to an arbitrator

00:05:59.359 --> 00:06:01.115
who can then impose an agreement.

00:06:01.115 --> 00:06:03.071
That doesn't actually get used a lot. 

00:06:03.071 --> 00:06:07.992
But the fact that that's the end point
completely shifts the incentive structure.

00:06:08.951 --> 00:06:11.954
U.S. policy allows plenty of opportunities
for delay.

00:06:11.954 --> 00:06:14.707
In Canada, the time between the petition
and the election is limited

00:06:14.707 --> 00:06:16.125
to 5 to 10 days.

00:06:16.125 --> 00:06:18.795
In the U.S., it takes 46 days on average.

00:06:19.337 --> 00:06:20.588
During that time, supervisors

00:06:20.588 --> 00:06:24.144
can pull workers into mandatory meetings
advising them to vote no.

00:06:24.144 --> 00:06:25.703
That's all legal.

00:06:25.703 --> 00:06:28.096
Firing workers for organizing is not.

00:06:28.721 --> 00:06:33.518
Coming out of college one of my first jobs
was as a manager in a warehouse

00:06:33.518 --> 00:06:37.063
and I got to see a little bit of what

00:06:37.063 --> 00:06:39.857
the anti-union kind of talk was all about.

00:06:40.483 --> 00:06:44.668
When someone that was hourly
just mentioned it

00:06:44.668 --> 00:06:48.282
and the general manager
put us into one of the operations

00:06:48.282 --> 00:06:51.452
manager's offices, it's a really small,
really crammed.

00:06:51.452 --> 00:06:55.164
We're all standing up and he just says,
if anyone says—

00:06:55.164 --> 00:06:56.707
can I like, curse? Because he cursed. 

00:06:56.707 --> 00:06:58.960
—Are you fine? Yeah, he said it.
—Absolutely.

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:03.131
He said, if anyone hears, 
the fucking word union.

00:07:03.131 --> 00:07:04.215
I want you to tell me.

00:07:04.215 --> 00:07:07.593
I want you to make sure that person
doesn't say it to anyone else.

00:07:07.593 --> 00:07:11.769
That just kind of stuck with me, and
everyone just accepted it for what it was.

00:07:11.769 --> 00:07:13.182
Was there any other talk of

00:07:13.182 --> 00:07:15.393
organizing among the workforce
that you were aware of?

00:07:15.393 --> 00:07:17.311
No, no.

00:07:17.311 --> 00:07:20.773
The person that mentioned organizing

00:07:20.773 --> 00:07:26.612
was fired, I want to say,
maybe four or five weeks later.

00:07:26.612 --> 00:07:30.533
There was a real effort to document
every single thing that he did

00:07:30.533 --> 00:07:32.618
that could be considered an infraction.

00:07:32.618 --> 00:07:35.204
So he could get written up.
We stacked those all up

00:07:35.204 --> 00:07:39.667
until he qualified to be fired 
and he was fired immediately.

00:07:39.667 --> 00:07:42.920
If an employer gets caught retaliating
against an organizer in the U.S.,

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:45.047
they have to "promise not to do it again",

00:07:45.047 --> 00:07:48.092
reinstate the worker, and award back pay.

00:07:48.092 --> 00:07:54.515
Minus whatever wages they had earned from
other employment that they had sought.

00:07:54.515 --> 00:07:57.894
It's not even the full amount that they
would have earned if they were on the job.

00:07:57.894 --> 00:08:00.938
And meanwhile, they send a message
to the other workers

00:08:00.938 --> 00:08:04.817
that, you know, this is what happens
when you try to unionize and they get

00:08:04.817 --> 00:08:09.091
to engage in the anti-union campaign
without a key leader in place.

00:08:09.697 --> 00:08:11.824
So it just makes no sense to obey the law.

00:08:12.325 --> 00:08:14.952
Since the 1930s, changes
to labor law in the U.S.

00:08:14.952 --> 00:08:18.748
have generally limited the tactics
unions can use, while making it easier

00:08:18.748 --> 00:08:21.334
for management to make their voice heard.

00:08:23.127 --> 00:08:25.087
But even if you change those laws,

00:08:25.087 --> 00:08:27.507
you still need workers
who are willing to fight.

00:08:27.507 --> 00:08:28.841
Hey, Cam it's Joss.

00:08:28.841 --> 00:08:32.220
Just sending over some news
out of New Jersey from yesterday.

00:08:32.261 --> 00:08:34.099
In case you didn't already see it.

00:08:36.849 --> 00:08:41.270
I scrolled through that thread
and read the article attached to it.

00:08:41.687 --> 00:08:46.734
And it looks like, you know, COVID,
maybe gave everyone a lot of perspective.

00:08:46.734 --> 00:08:49.111
At least that's what it seems like
from reading that article.

00:08:49.111 --> 00:08:51.489
But really interesting.
Thank you for sending that.

00:08:51.489 --> 00:08:52.573
The last question I had

00:08:52.573 --> 00:08:54.599
when I saw the union decline is

00:08:54.599 --> 00:08:56.744
do people actually want to unionize? 

00:08:56.744 --> 00:08:58.120
There's not a ton of data on this,

00:08:58.120 --> 00:09:02.291
but public approval of unions right now
is higher than it's been since the 1960s

00:09:02.291 --> 00:09:06.587
and a nationally representative survey
in 2017 found that 48% of people

00:09:06.587 --> 00:09:10.216
who were not in a union said
they'd vote for one at their workplace.

00:09:10.216 --> 00:09:12.260
48% is here.

00:09:13.010 --> 00:09:14.887
That's a tough gap to close.

00:09:14.887 --> 00:09:18.015
But with the pandemic as a catalyst
and the unemployment rate low...

00:09:18.599 --> 00:09:21.143
unions are having a go at it in 2022.

00:09:21.811 --> 00:09:24.730
One of the effects of union decline
just means that a lot of people

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:28.168
just don't know people in unions
don't know what unions do.

00:09:28.168 --> 00:09:33.014
So then the effect of these 
kind of breakthroughs

00:09:33.014 --> 00:09:35.741
is that it changes people's consciousness

00:09:35.741 --> 00:09:40.538
by making those options more realistic
and something that, like,

00:09:40.538 --> 00:09:45.585
I could actually take a risk
and actually try to win this thing

00:09:45.585 --> 00:09:49.456
and I might actually have a
decent chance of getting it.

00:09:51.524 --> 00:09:53.009
So that's everything that I found.

00:09:53.009 --> 00:09:55.303
It's more complicated than I hoped.

00:09:55.303 --> 00:10:00.016
But are there any questions
you have about the history or any of that?

00:10:00.488 --> 00:10:02.351
No, I don't have any questions.

00:10:02.351 --> 00:10:05.062
It's really—it's been like really exciting

00:10:05.062 --> 00:10:10.194
to kind of go through this and see 
all the work that goes into it.

00:10:10.194 --> 00:10:11.444
Yeah, it's awesome.

00:10:11.444 --> 00:10:13.988
We really appreciate
you sending in the question.

00:10:13.988 --> 00:10:16.664
We're going to use this as like
a pilot episode

00:10:16.664 --> 00:10:18.534
to try to get more people 
to send us questions.

00:10:18.534 --> 00:10:23.497
So hopefully people will see it
and want to do what you did.

